WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:02.350
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:05.269
In this lesson, we're going to take a look at MPCs

3
00:00:05.269 --> 00:00:08.470
and how they work overall and why using them.

4
00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:09.949
And then in the following video,

5
00:00:09.949 --> 00:00:12.669
we'll actually break it down and make one ourselves.

6
00:00:12.669 --> 00:00:14.470
So let's get started.

7
00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:16.250
So first up, many people wonder

8
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.570
what is a material parameter collection?

9
00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.390
Well, what they are is they allow us to go in here

10
00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.410
and to be able to adjust things sweepingly in a material

11
00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:25.710
to be able to control the properties

12
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.030
and the look and feel.

13
00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:30.170
They can range from anything from making flowers die

14
00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.890
to changing the color of them

15
00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.049
or to make them look like they're more wet.

16
00:00:34.049 --> 00:00:35.609
I'm going to show you the example that I have,

17
00:00:35.609 --> 00:00:38.929
the MPCs active and a little bit about how they work

18
00:00:38.929 --> 00:00:41.369
in the scene and then in the following video,

19
00:00:41.369 --> 00:00:43.210
we'll actually do it ourselves.

20
00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.789
So let's go and jump into Unreal and take a look.

21
00:00:45.789 --> 00:00:48.409
So here in Unreal, we have a scene set up

22
00:00:48.409 --> 00:00:52.840
and this is using the MPC underscore mat.

23
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.200
If you're wondering where that's at,

24
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.000
go into your class files

25
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.320
and you'll see on your class files under maps,

26
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.359
you'll see the MPC mat.

27
00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:03.520
That's the one that I have open here.

28
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:08.159
We're going to also go ahead and open up our cinematics.

29
00:01:08.159 --> 00:01:10.879
So let's go to contents, open up cinematics folder

30
00:01:10.879 --> 00:01:11.719
and we're going to go ahead

31
00:01:11.719 --> 00:01:15.000
and open up our MPC simple animation.

32
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.640
In here, we can go ahead and hit play

33
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.260
and as we hit play,

34
00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.120
we can actually see things change over time.

35
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.560
I'm just simply sliding over the bar

36
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.480
and you can see the materials get wetter

37
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:31.439
and the rain appear in the opacity here.

38
00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:36.090
Now, this is all being able to be controlled via an MPC.

39
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:39.400
We can add whatever actors that we need

40
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.640
and then we can also, when we do that,

41
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.700
load a material parameter collection

42
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.719
connected to that actor, as you see in my menu.

43
00:01:48.719 --> 00:01:52.000
The MPCs, if we go to that folder where they're found,

44
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.859
allow us to be able to affect the materials

45
00:01:53.859 --> 00:01:56.079
the way that we need.

46
00:01:56.079 --> 00:01:57.420
Now, if we go to these objects,

47
00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.980
let's actually pick on the statue here

48
00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:03.609
and we go ahead and go to where it's found in the scene.

49
00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:05.269
We're going to double click on the material

50
00:02:05.269 --> 00:02:06.689
that we have in place

51
00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:10.250
and you'll see that we have one called MPC here.

52
00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.870
The MPCs are nested in this material

53
00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:17.250
but we also have the MPC built right alongside it.

54
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.069
If we double click on the MPC,

55
00:02:19.069 --> 00:02:22.229
you'll see that we have an area here for scalar

56
00:02:22.229 --> 00:02:24.069
to be able to control range

57
00:02:24.069 --> 00:02:27.150
and we have vector if we wanted to control color.

58
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.270
We'll do this in the other video

59
00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:31.490
where we'll actually build both of these out.

60
00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:34.370
So if we go into our scene itself,

61
00:02:34.409 --> 00:02:37.479
let me minimize this for a second

62
00:02:37.479 --> 00:02:39.860
and we go ahead and take a look at this material,

63
00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:41.520
the MPC one.

64
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.439
I'm going to click on the magnifying glass for it

65
00:02:43.439 --> 00:02:45.919
and just double click on that material.

66
00:02:45.919 --> 00:02:49.759
You'll see that we have a global roughness high

67
00:02:49.759 --> 00:02:53.280
and it's driving our multiply here.

68
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:58.560
And we also have an MPC here for global roughness low

69
00:03:00.759 --> 00:03:04.060
and wetness both punched into a multiply

70
00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.879
which actually can work pretty well.

71
00:03:06.439 --> 00:03:08.919
This just allows us to control the range on or off

72
00:03:08.919 --> 00:03:11.520
in a particular if we want to go high or low

73
00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.120
with this particular look.

74
00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.699
Now I'm using it in the specular.

75
00:03:14.699 --> 00:03:16.960
You don't have to, but you do have to be careful

76
00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:18.740
and I'll show this in the next video

77
00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.259
between specular and roughness

78
00:03:20.259 --> 00:03:22.560
because one can turn the other one off.

79
00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.520
That's something you just have to be aware of

80
00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.659
when working with Unreal.

81
00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:29.300
You'll notice that our global roughness high

82
00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:30.819
is punched into the A channel.

83
00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:33.180
You can also punch that into the B channel

84
00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.930
and in here we have a roughness texture set in place.

85
00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:40.810
Now these now, once they're loaded in Sequencer

86
00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.650
allow us to be able to control the look and feel

87
00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:47.210
of our objects in our scene.

88
00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:48.530
Well, that's it with this video.

89
00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.729
In the next video, we're going to go and build these out

90
00:03:50.729 --> 00:03:52.650
and you're going to see them applied in this scene

91
00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.229
and see how they work.

92
00:03:54.229 --> 00:03:55.069
Thanks again.
